WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:57.130
Venezuela has the world's largest proven oil reserves.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.556
And yet the country has completely run out of gasoline.

00:01:01.556 --> 00:01:04.659
After nearly two decades of epic mismanagement,

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:08.930
the socialist government no longer has the capacity to extract oil from the ground 

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:12.850
or to refine it into usable form.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.214
The shortages have paralyzed the economy.

00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:18.750
Travel within the country has become prohibitively expensive.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.530
Farmers can barely afford to bring their crops to the cities.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.927
Meanwhile, price restrictions have given rise to a vibrant black market.

00:01:27.927 --> 00:01:32.380
Andrés, a public bus operator in Caracas, is given special access to fill up his tank

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.659
 because he provides an essential city service, but earning the equivalent of under $200 a month,

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:41.609
he's struggling to make ends meet.

00:01:41.609 --> 00:01:45.000
Bus operators in his position often prefer to keep their bus parked

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.629
and extract gas from the tank to resell on the black market for about eight dollars per gallon.

00:01:50.629 --> 00:01:54.077
To put that into perspective, the average Venezuelan individual 

00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:57.213
subsists on less than five dollars per day.

00:03:29.922 --> 00:03:34.897
The little gas that is still available comes via periodic shipments from Iran, 

00:03:34.897 --> 00:03:38.530
but the Venezuelan government barely charges for that gasoline.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.859
Instead it rations it through a quota system.

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:51.225
So filling up your tank can mean waiting in
line for days.

00:03:51.225 --> 00:03:56.140
David used to be a car mechanic, but now he charges
clients for the time spent waiting in lines

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:01.063
at subsidized gas stations in order to fill
up their tanks with cheaper fuel.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.712
Before 2003, Venezuela state owned oil company, known as Pedevesa, worked closely with foreign multinationals 

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:49.424
and was mostly insulated from government interference.

00:07:33.545 --> 00:07:37.090
Hugo Chavez became the president of Venezuela
in 1999.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:41.930
And up until his death from cancer in 2013,
he would enact the policy agenda that he dubbed

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:47.013
"Socialism for the 21st Century," turning one
of the most prosperous countries in Latin America

00:07:47.013 --> 00:07:49.369
into the site of a humanitarian crisis.

00:07:49.369 --> 00:07:53.147
Chavez rewrote the constitution, clamped down on freedom of the press, 

00:07:53.147 --> 00:07:55.539
nationalized over a thousand private companies, 

00:07:55.539 --> 00:07:58.639
and destroyed the national currency through hyperinflation.

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:04.770
When Chavez attempted to seize control of Pedevesa in 2002, the employees staged the massive strike.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:10.932
So Chavez fired 18,000 state oil workers,
including 80% of its top engineers.

00:09:52.519 --> 00:09:58.610
In yet another tragic irony of a country ruined by socialism, Chaves' handpicked successor,

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:03.957
Venezuelan dictator Nicholas Maduro, is now working to bring privately run foreign oil companies 

00:10:03.957 --> 00:10:10.345
back into the country to revive the industry.

00:10:10.345 --> 00:10:15.573
Meanwhile, in a tragic reminder that the most
valuable commodity isn't a natural resource,

00:10:15.573 --> 00:10:18.293
but the human expertise to put it to productive use, 

00:10:18.293 --> 00:10:21.167
Venezuela the country with the most oil in the world,

00:10:21.167 --> 00:10:24.104
is paralyzed because it has run out of gasoline.

